Q1: My pick (a) adopt. Rules:
1. #1 Reckon before battle: the reckoning needs the owner's facts; trials add 算 only where the count depends on them. If no trial can change the answer, more trials add no 算 — 無算. Also "索其情" — search out the true situation, which lives with the owner.
2. #4 Know whether you can fight: 「知可以戰與不可以戰者勝」 — knowing which questions can be settled by a trial and which cannot is the same discrimination. #4's half-victory: knowing your troops can attack but not the ground = half. Sending a question to trials that cannot answer it is a half-victory at best.
3. #17/#11: probe learns what responds to action; #11 says foreknowledge 「不可驗於度」 and 「必取於人，知敵之情者也」 — some knowledge must come from the person who holds it, not from measurement. A location a requirement names, whether a real project exists, a threshold — these are in EJ's keeping.
4. #8 value victory not duration: three trials of an unanswerable question is 久 — 兵聞拙速，未睹巧之久也. Cost: time-boxed runs burn their clock.
5. #5 move only on advantage: the three-trial rule had a purpose — not to interrupt the owner on every small thing. Adopting (a) must keep a filter: send at once only if the question is one no trial can answer, and only after the run has checked that (one trial/source check), so the owner isn't flooded. This is the harm side of #6.
6. #2 make yourself unbeatable first — 無恃其不來，恃吾有以待之: don't build the piece on the hope that a third trial will settle it; build on the owner's answer or leave it open.
7. #13 君命有所不受 — hmm, the general may not accept the ruler's command when the way of battle says otherwise. Does that argue against at-once escalation? No; the owner is the ruler here, and #13 is about a general on the ground with knowledge the ruler lacks — it cuts the other way: the run's main agent may decide provisionally and not wait, which runs support via 3.8 item 14. Could use #13 for the time-boxed variant: in a time-boxed run the main agent decides provisionally (its own judgement on the ground, 君命有所不受) and lists it for the owner. That's a nice fit: (a) in a time-boxed run = decide provisionally at once, list for the owner, which is exactly #13's shape: act on your own read of the situation, do not seek fame or avoid blame. Hmm, but 君命有所不受 is about the ruler's command to fight, not about information. It's a bit of a stretch but supports "the run decides provisionally rather than stalling."

Also #6 harm/benefit mixing: adopting (a) has a harm — the owner's attention is a scarce resource; the rule must say what a "question no trial can answer" is, or every unclear spot gets sent "at once" and the owner's box fills. So propose a gate: at once means (i) the run has stated which trial could answer it, and shown that trial cannot (one check, not three), or (ii) the answer belongs to the owner by definition (location, existence, threshold). That's a "proposed change to the pattern" — actually it's a refinement of the option. Label it clearly.

Wait, the pattern bullet says: "A question goes to EJ only after three failed trials on it or once the time is used up." If I pick (a) that is a *proposed change to the pattern*. The brief says label such points "proposed change to the pattern" and say what it would change. I must do that for Q1, since adopting (a) changes that pattern bullet. Good — I should explicitly label it.

Cost/risk of (a): owner interruptions; questions that look unanswerable but aren't (a lazy agent sends everything); owner becomes the bottleneck; the three-trial rule protected against premature escalation. Mitigations: name the class of questions (owner-only facts: location named, existence of a real project, threshold) + require the run to state what trial it would have tried; batch questions at the next natural checkpoint in a non-time-boxed run.

Also "what I need to know": for Q1 — is the owner willing to be the single point for three classes of question at any hour? Does "at once" mean immediately out-of-band or at the next checkpoint? In a time-boxed run, "at once" = decide provisionally at once and list. The brief already says that. For Q2 — does the owner consider Claude Code's harness memory in scope for the tool (privacy: MEMORY.md holds personal facts like EJ's investing context; reporting a count is fine, but listing names could leak)? That's a real cost: the folder holds personal facts (user context). Listing it present/absent + a count of children is harmless; listing names (as agentMemory() does for agent folders) could expose personal facts. Also F1 says "No file inside a location is read to decide it" (locations.ts:1-2), so present/absent is consistent.

