So Q1: a question no trial can answer. The existing rule counts a *trial* as "one provisional choice tried and checked." If the question isn't a choice that can be tried (e.g., which location a requirement names), there is no trial to run — so "after three failed trials" never even starts. That's an important reading: the rule is undefined for questions with no trial. So adopting (a) is not really an exception to the three-trial rule; it names the case the rule doesn't cover. That's a strong argument: it fills a gap rather than overriding a check. Let me verify the definition at line 555-556: "A trial is one provisional choice tried and checked; one that fails its check is a failed trial (logged, R12.2), and the next trial takes the experts' next option."

